<<~ ahu slot=`#/2-the-forks-re-read-under-the-rulings`>>

!! 2. The forks, re-read under the rulings

!!! 2.1 Q-A2: the plugin library and a person's own fetching code ride an approved crossing

''The ruling's words.'' A person may load a plugin library, run their own custom code that fetches from the internet, or ask their node to fetch local resources. Such reaches ride an approved crossing under the Web3-only law's causal-island boundary, never a silent page fetch.

''What the page realm can and cannot enforce.'' A plugin running in the mirror's page realm calls `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket` and `document.createElement("iframe")` as freely as house code. No house listener sits between it and the network. The one platform lever that governs //every// page-realm reach, house or plugin, is the document's ''Content-Security-Policy'': `connect-src` for fetch/XHR/WebSocket/EventSource, `frame-src` for the library iframe. A reach outside the policy fails, and the failure fires a `securitypolicyviolation` event on the document naming the blocked URI and the directive. That turns a silent fetch into a ''named refusal'', which is the whole of what the door law asks for at this layer.

''The design (slice M3).''
# ''A face carries a crossing list'' as an intent on its face (<code>&#64;intents-&lt;tag></code>, `project_face_intent_plane`): `crossings: [{origin, kinds: ["connect" | "frame"]}]`. A fresh face carries none. The plugin-library preset (`https://tiddlywiki.com`, kind `frame`) is a //one-tap// addition the person makes from the alert, never a default.
# ''The surface fences the realm before any plugin runs.'' The mirror boot asks the vessel (over the port) for the worn face's crossing list, composes one CSP, and inserts it as a `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` ''before'' `TW5Engine.boot` (`glass-mirror.ts:45`) and before the engine evaluates any module. Base policy: `default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' <vessel port origin> <face crossings:connect>; frame-src <face crossings:frame>; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval'` (TW5 evaluates modules with `new Function`, `boot.js:617`; the engine blob itself is CID-named, so `'unsafe-eval'` names the plugin model, not a hole). Where the page is served by the node, the node //also// sends the same policy as a header (header and meta compose; the stricter wins). On a civic-served anon vessel, the meta alone fences.
# ''A blocked reach lands on the alert rail'': `document.addEventListener("securitypolicyviolation", …)` writes `$:/temp/lares/alert/refused/crossing/<origin>` with the directive and the blocked URI, plus the one-tap "allow `<origin>` for this face" action that writes the intent. The next ''clean-realm reload'' (K3) carries the widened fence.
# ''`browser-messaging` stays stock.'' `tm-load-plugin-library` opens its hidden iframe; with the origin on the face's `frame` list the library loads, the plugin lands by `$tw.wiki.addTiddler` (`browser-messaging.js:196`), the port adaptor posts it as a write, and it activates on the next boot, where the ''A3 census'' refuses a `syncadaptor`/`saver` carrier by name. Without the origin on the list the iframe navigation fails by `frame-src`, and the alert names it.
# ''`tm-http-request` crosses as the `http-request` door (D15, `dreamdom-roadmap.mem:212`)'' — unchanged. The door runs in the vessel (worker or node) under the same face list, with private ranges refused //at the page//, and the response landing as a sensed snapshot. The node-box setup's "ask my node to fetch a local resource" rides this door on the node under the node's own list (§6 Q2 names the one open piece).
# ''The Web3 library road'' (owed, not in this lane): a plugin library is an HTML page that answers `postMessage({verb:"GET", url:"recipes/library/tiddlers.json"})`. The house can serve that shape from the vessel's own origin over the offering plane (`nexus publish`, `dreamnet-architecture.mem` #/publication-and-discovery), so `tm-load-plugin-library` works ''unchanged'' against a Web3 source with no crossing at all. That is the people-customize road the ruling asked about.

''What it does not cure, named.'' A plugin may still exfiltrate to an origin the person //has// allowed. The fence names the trust boundary; it does not read intent. That matches "a person's own custom code that fetches" being pono.

!!! 2.2 Q-B1 and Q-B3: does the civic DNS design make a custody origin servable, and does it change Safari?

''The DNS design, re-read.'' One stable name per reach face, earned by a Let's Encrypt DNS-01 ''wildcard'' over a zone the serving entity holds a delegation cap on, served from a terminator in front of the vessel, with a split-horizon resolver on the LAN (`secure-context.mem` #/why-dns-01, #/behind-the-terminator). The civic layer: the first Nexus domains (`amorphousdreams.net`, `elyncia.net`), `op1.amorphousdreams.net` as the zero-friction on-ramp with the seat bound to the ''node key'', swappable to an own domain later (`project_civic_tls_at_scale` §⑧); per-operator delegated DCV where the operator holds their own ACME account (§⑦); a PSL submission at founding. "Inversion of control" reads here as: the Nexus holds the apex and delegates names and challenges downward; identity never passes through a name (`secure-context.mem` #/name-and-key).

''Servable on every setup — yes, and nearly free.''
* A wildcard `*.home.op1.tld` already covers `custody.home.op1.tld` beside `freyja.home.op1.tld`: one more label, zero extra certificates, zero extra DNS records, one more vhost on the terminator. The spec's "costs a second origin (DNS-01 and TLS)" objection ''dissolves''.
* Node box (setup 1): the node serves both labels. Anon-device vessel and user-class cap-stack with no node (setups 2, 4): the civic Nexus's static host serves `app.<nexus>` and `custody.<nexus>`. Fleet (3): each vessel pairs the same way.

''But the platform kills both custody-origin shapes on the phone seat.''
* ''K-frame'' (the Privy shape, custody in a hidden cross-origin or sibling-subdomain iframe): on Safari that frame's IndexedDB is ''partitioned and ephemeral'', Storage Access grants cookies only (§1 row 5). A vessel whose slot tree lives there loses it when Safari quits. The Home Screen exemption lifts the 7-day cap for the //first-party// domain and does nothing for the frame's ephemerality. Chrome and Firefox: fine (partitioned but persistent under one top-level site). One engine out of three, and it is the family's.
* ''K-popup'' (the Coinbase shape, custody in a top-level popup): inside an iOS Home Screen web app `window.open()` returns ''null'' (§1 row 7). The one phone seat that keeps its storage past seven days cannot open the ceremony. In a Safari tab the popup works, but that tab's storage dies in seven idle days, and Safari drops the opening gesture across the WebAuthn `await` (§1b row 33).
* So ''a custody origin fails exactly where the house most needs it'', whatever DNS serves it. The spec's ⚑ on K-frame holds and widens to K-popup.

''What the DNS design //does// change: the rpId's home.'' A passkey slot binds to an rpId, and an rpId is a name. Under "on-ramp name now, own domain later", a passkey slot bound to `app.op1.amorphousdreams.net` stops opening when the vessel moves to `freyja.example`. Two levers, both DNS-side: (a) ''WebAuthn Related Origin Requests'' (`/.well-known/webauthn` on the rpId's origin listing the origins allowed to use it; §1b row 30) lets a civic rpId (`amorphousdreams.net`) serve a vessel from the operator's own domain, up to the label limit; (b) the slot tree already holds several slots, so a name move re-binds the passkey slot by the paper or passphrase route. Lean: the passkey slot's rpId is ''the vessel's served origin'', and a name move is a re-bind act; Related Origins stay an offer the civic Nexus may light. §6 Q7.

''Does it change Safari?'' No. Nothing in DNS reaches ITP's partition or the Home Screen `open()` rule. It does settle one Safari detail in R's favour: ITP's 7-day counter and partition key run per ''registrable domain'' (§1b row 31), so an app and any sibling label under one operator name share one counter — daily use of the wiki keeps a sibling alive. That helps only a design that has a sibling, which R does not need.

''The verdict on Q-B1.'' ''R stands as the end-state'': the ask runs in a clean realm (no person's code has run), the daily secret is a passkey PRF under the app origin's rpId, passphrase and paper are the recovery slots, the opened key lives in the dedicated worker, and lock-and-re-ask is a navigation. Residuals, now sharper: (a) a plugin may call `credentials.get()` ''without any gesture'' (§1 row 2.5) and receive the PRF output if the person approves the real OS prompt while the wiki is painted; (b) a fake passphrase box; (c) the served-page hole. The plain-words rule ("if your wiki shows, any ask is fake") is the guard for (a) and (b), and §6 Q3 asks whether the operator wants the platform to guard instead (lift the Mu for the custody path alone).

''Q-B3, the holder.'' The DNS design does not bear on it. `?holder` burns (a URL-selected topology). The dedicated worker stays the shipped topology. The only future in which a SharedWorker holder returns is a custody-origin vessel, where that origin's SharedWorker //is// the one-vessel-per-site holder; the phone verdict above parks that future. Nothing to rule now beyond confirming the burn.

!!! 2.3 What the DNS re-read adds to the mirror lane

* The page's CSP (§2.1) is served by the node on setup 1 and carried as a meta on setups 2-4. Both compose. The ''vessel port origin'' on `connect-src` is the `wss://` reach face (`LAR_PUBLIC_URL`'s scheme travels with the setting, `secure-context.mem:83`).
* The mirror never derives the vessel's name from a request; it reads it off the port grant (`port:open`), the same handle that carries width (`MIRROR DEFAULT`).

