<<~ ahu slot=`#/1b-the-crossing-fence-facts`>>

!! 1b. The crossing-fence facts (rows 25-33)

| # | Claim v2 leans on | Verdict | Evidence |h
| 25 | `connect-src` governs `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `EventSource`, `sendBeacon` (also `fetchLater()` and `<a ping>`); `worker-src` falls back to `child-src` → `script-src` → `default-src` | ✔ | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/connect-src; …/worker-src |
| 26 | A script-inserted `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` is enforced from its insertion onward; it never loosens; `frame-ancestors`, `report-uri`, `sandbox` and report-only are not honoured in meta | ✔ | https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/ §3.3 "policies in meta elements are not applied to content which precedes them"; MDN CSP header: "Adding additional policies can only further restrict the capabilities of the protected resource"; https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors "not supported in the `<meta>` element" |
| 27 | `securitypolicyviolation` fires on `Document`/`Element`/worker with `blockedURI`, `effectiveDirective`, `disposition`; Baseline since 2018 (Chrome 41 · Firefox 63 · Safari 10) | ✔ | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/SecurityPolicyViolationEvent |
| 28 | A same-origin `https:` worker script takes its ''own'' CSP from its response headers, not the document's; `blob:`/`data:` workers inherit the creator's | ✔ | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Using_web_workers "not governed by the content security policy of the document … that created them … The exception … scheme of data or blob" |
| 29 | Page → private-range fetch: Chrome's ''Local Network Access'' permission prompt ships in Chrome 142 (RFC 1918, link-local, loopback); Firefox 145 ships a prompt + policy; Safari/WebKit has the check algorithm with no prompt (treated as Denied) | ✔ (Safari 18 "local network privacy" for web content: ? likely false) | https://developer.chrome.com/blog/local-network-access; https://firefox-admin-docs.mozilla.org/reference/policies/localnetworkaccess/; https://www2.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=319907 |
| 30 | WebAuthn Related Origin Requests: `https://<rpId>/.well-known/webauthn` `{origins:[…]}`; Chrome 128, Safari 18 (iOS 18); Firefox not shipped (Jan 2026); at least 5 labels, no client beyond 5; feature-detect `relatedOrigins` in `getClientCapabilities()` | ✔ | https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/public-webauthn-adoption/2024Jul/0003.html; https://web.dev/articles/webauthn-related-origin-requests; https://passkeys.dev/docs/advanced/related-origins/ |
| 31 | ITP's "website" is the registrable domain with all subdomains; interaction = click, tap or keyboard entry; the 7-day cap counts days of Safari use; Home Screen apps keep their own counter | ✔ | https://webkit.org/tracking-prevention/ "A website is a registrable domain including all of its subdomains"; https://webkit.org/blog/10218/full-third-party-cookie-blocking-and-more/ |
| 32 | `postMessage` between opener and popup across origins; a `MessagePort` transfers cross-origin; `COOP: same-origin` severs the opener, `same-origin-allow-popups` keeps it | ✔ | https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/postMessage; https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy |
| 33 | Safari blocks `window.open` after an async gap in the click handler (a WebAuthn `await` included); a fix landed 2025-12-01 whose shipped version is unconfirmed; transient activation lasts "at most a few seconds" (the 5 s figure ?) | ✔ in substance | https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=302803; https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Transient_activation; https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html |

''What rows 25-33 settle.'' The fence works in every engine, from a meta, before the engine boots (26, 27). It fences the ''page realm only'': the vessel's dedicated worker keeps its own policy (28), so the D15 door's fetch in the worker runs under house code, not the page's fence, which is the shape the door law wants. A plugin cannot spawn a `blob:` worker past `worker-src 'self'` to escape the fence (25, 28). A page's own reach into the LAN now meets a browser prompt on Chrome and Firefox and a silent deny on Safari (29), which makes the ''node's'' door the only pono road to "ask my node to fetch a local resource" (§6 Q2). Row 33 adds a third strike against K-popup: the ceremony's `await` on WebAuthn sits between the tap and any `window.open`.

